class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mord mathrm\">O</span><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Sunlight</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel x-arrow\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.622em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.144em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.522em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight x-arrow-pad\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"></span></span></span><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-2.511em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.522em;\"></span><span class=\"hide-tail\" style=\"height:0.522em;min-width:1.469em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width='400em' height='0.522em' viewBox='0 0 400000 522' preserveAspectRatio='xMaxYMin slice'><path d='M0 241v40h399891c-47.3 35.3-84 78-110 128\n-16.7 32-27.7 63.7-33 95 0 1.3-.2 2.7-.5 4-.3 1.3-.5 2.3-.5 3 0 7.3 6.7 11 20\n 11 8 0 13.2-.8 15.5-2.5 2.3-1.7 4.2-5.5 5.5-11.5 2-13.3 5.7-27 11-41 14.7-44.7\n 39-84.5 73-119.5s73.7-60.2 119-75.5c6-2 9-5.7 9-11s-3-9-9-11c-45.3-15.3-85\n-40.5-119-75.5s-58.3-74.8-73-119.5c-4.7-14-8.3-27.3-11-40-1.3-6.7-3.2-10.8-5.5\n-12.5-2.3-1.7-7.5-2.5-15.5-2.5-14 0-21 3.7-21 11 0 2 2 10.3 6 25 20.7 83.3 67\n 151.7 139 205zm0 0v40h399900v-40z'/></svg></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.011em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Sugars</span></span><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"></span><span class=\"mord mathrm\">O</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord rlap\"><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\" style=\"color:transparent;\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.07847em;color:transparent;\">X</span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:-0.15em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.7em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>","enumerator":"1","key":"L4lTKwSE2i"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the geological carbon cycle, a portion of the biological-cycle carbon becomes part of the geological carbon cycle: plant materials into coal and petroleum, tiny fragments and molecules into organic-rich shale, and the carbonate bearing calcareous shells and other parts of marine organisms into limestone. Such materials become buried and become part of the slow geologic formation of coal and other sedimentary materials. This cycle actually involves most of Earth’s carbon and operates very slowly.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"foZMKGPaoE"}],"key":"kRA5XNs90X"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Greenhouse Effect","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"lRqVfF9fbC"}],"identifier":"greenhouse-effect","label":"Greenhouse Effect","html_id":"greenhouse-effect","implicit":true,"key":"o9IDTP50eG"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The greenhouse effect is the reason our global temperature is rising, but it’s important to understand what this effect is and how it occurs. The greenhouse effect occurs because greenhouse gases are present in the atmosphere. The greenhouse effect is named after a similar process that warms a greenhouse or a car on a hot summer day. Sunlight passes through the glass of the greenhouse or car, reaches the interior, and changes into heat. The heat radiates upward and gets trapped by the glass windows. The greenhouse effect for the Earth can be explained in three steps.","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"ouNP162BmA"}],"key":"XWcOjc66wn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 1: Solar radiation from the sun is composed of mostly ultraviolet (UV), visible light, and infrared (IR) radiation. Components of solar radiation include parts with a shorter wavelength than visible light, like ultraviolet light, and parts of the spectrum with longer wavelengths, like IR and others. Some of the radiation gets absorbed, scattered, or reflected by the atmospheric gases but about half of the solar radiation eventually reaches the Earth’s surface.","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"t5iAo6IZOw"}],"key":"dqf2VE4OA2"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/0e75ae1ce1fc5c15cd82cf6cbe8d82d6.png","alt":"Simulated spectral solar irradiance at the top of the atmosphere (ToA) and the Earth’s surface. The different regions of the spectrum include: ultraviolet (UV), visible, near infrared (NIR), and shortwave infrared (SWIR). Image source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"yCktcMh0Ui","urlSource":"https://earth.gsfc.nasa.gov/sites/default/files/lab_climate/tsis-diagram_annotated.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"solarspectrum","identifier":"solarspectrum","html_id":"solarspectrum","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"DbhAyHTjxY"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"o9YxsTceR2"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"G5KiuhXEwe"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"OuRH0AfYpU"},{"type":"text","value":"Simulated spectral solar irradiance at the top of the atmosphere (ToA) and the Earth’s surface. The different regions of the spectrum include: ultraviolet (UV), visible, near infrared (NIR), and shortwave infrared (SWIR). ","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"LPMhKWOuTw"},{"type":"link","url":"https://earth.gsfc.nasa.gov/climate/projects/solar-irradiance/science","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Image source","position":{"start":{"line":54,"column":1},"end":{"line":54,"column":1}},"key":"YXIKofvPvv"}],"urlSource":"https://earth.gsfc.nasa.gov/climate/projects/solar-irradiance/science","key":"RezpGp2GhB"}],"key":"tfkHpiVZsS"}],"key":"tFBA8BM9Dl"}],"class":"margin-caption","label":"solarspectrum","identifier":"solarspectrum","enumerator":"1","html_id":"solarspectrum","key":"jAANg3yoKn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 2: The visible, UV, and IR radiation, that reaches the surface converts to heat energy. Most students have experienced sunlight warming a surface such as pavement, a patio, or deck. When this occurs, the warmer surface then emits thermal radiation, which is a type of IR radiation. So, there is a conversion from visible, UV, and IR to just thermal IR. This thermal IR is what we experience as heat. If you have ever felt heat radiating from a fire or a hot stove top, then you have experienced thermal IR.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"x8BH7xZQaJ"}],"key":"encd6lmDLM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Step 3: Thermal IR radiates from the earth’s surface back into the atmosphere. But since it is thermal IR instead of UV, visible, or regular IR, this thermal IR gets trapped by greenhouse gases. In other words, the sun’s energy leaves the Earth at a different wavelength than it enters, so the sun’s energy is not absorbed in the lower atmosphere when energy is coming in, but rather when the energy is going out. The gases that are mostly responsible for this energy blocking on Earth include carbon dioxide, water vapor, methane, and nitrous oxide. More greenhouse gases in the atmosphere results in more thermal IR being trapped.","position":{"start":{"line":59,"column":1},"end":{"line":59,"column":1}},"key":"yN6WYrqybZ"}],"key":"ooNxweCSyA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://opengeology.org/textbook/15-global-climate-change/#1522_Carbon_Dioxide","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text Source","position":{"start":{"line":61,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"key":"jC2ZH1vzfr"}],"urlSource":"https://opengeology.org/textbook/15-global-climate-change/#1522_Carbon_Dioxide","key":"SFL7I0hnz6"}],"key":"x5oMmRpGvL"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Climate Change Evidence","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"GWHOPMkuZ4"}],"identifier":"climate-change-evidence","label":"Climate Change Evidence","html_id":"climate-change-evidence","implicit":true,"key":"Yjs5pCzSwn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"While climate has changed often in the past due to natural causes, the scientific consensus is that human activity is causing current very rapid climate change. While this seems like a new idea, it was suggested more than 75 years ago. This section describes the evidence of what most scientists agree is anthropogenic or human-caused climate change.","position":{"start":{"line":65,"column":1},"end":{"line":65,"column":1}},"key":"hk5TCo4tw0"}],"key":"ELoxC2aSCq"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/dquECwUfIQg","width":"100%","key":"DXt7kFHUdD"}],"key":"qERA3AWx58"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global Temp Rise","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"key":"wxLo7C4UjW"}],"identifier":"global-temp-rise","label":"Global Temp Rise","html_id":"global-temp-rise","implicit":true,"key":"rrXhFRhCqO"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":75,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The land-ocean temperature index, 1880 to present, compared to a base reference time of 1951-1980, shows ocean temperatures steadily rising. The solid black line is the global annual mean, and the solid red line is the five-year Lowess smoothing. The blue uncertainty bars (95 percent confidence limit) account only for incomplete spatial sampling.","position":{"start":{"line":75,"column":1},"end":{"line":75,"column":1}},"key":"Bxz0gaBxCf"}],"key":"i4W4QTbn6D"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"link","url":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/15-global-climate-change/","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text Source","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"ai7OQX2Zpd"}],"urlSource":"https://slcc.pressbooks.pub/introgeology/chapter/15-global-climate-change/","key":"mf7TaRBnIp"}],"key":"nK9zRhidZw"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/329043ef48fbf993382cde1c093089c7.png","alt":"Land-ocean temperature index, 1880 to present, with a base time 1951-1980. The solid black line is the global annual mean and the solid red line is the five-year Lowess smoothing. The blue uncertainty bars (95% confidence limit) account only for incomplete spatial sampling. The graph shows that Earth’s temperature is rising. Image source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"dc3J4lH8Bi","urlSource":"https://data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/graphs_v4/graph_data/Temperature_Anomalies_over_Land_and_over_Ocean/graph.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"globalmeantemp","identifier":"globalmeantemp","html_id":"globalmeantemp","enumerator":"2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"zVvncuJfcI"},{"type":"text"