re—since the 1940’s when quality records for glaciers began.","position":{"start":{"line":103,"column":1},"end":{"line":103,"column":1}},"key":"jv2wNywXoD"}],"key":"eArlM5M9jB"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":105,"column":1},"end":{"line":105,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before anthropogenic warming, glacial activity was variable with some retreating and some advancing. Now, spring snow cover is decreasing, and sea ice is shrinking. Most sea ice is at the North Pole, which is only occupied by the Arctic Ocean and sea ice. The NOAA animation shows how perennial sea ice has declined from 1987 to 2015. The oldest ice is white, and the youngest, seasonal ice is dark blue. The amount of old ice has declined from 20 percent in 1985 to 3 percent in 2015.","position":{"start":{"line":105,"column":1},"end":{"line":105,"column":1}},"key":"geG8Ovk6aJ"}],"key":"rwgpqReqdR"},{"type":"div","class":"container","children":[{"type":"iframe","class":"video","src":"https://www.youtube.com/embed/9X07X35FzUc","width":"100%","key":"OQ8aEtTJpP"}],"key":"XIVnohUOnQ"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"/LandIceAntarctica-ee60e004568632cd3a52715f6c026c05.png","alt":"Decline of Antarctic ice mass from 2002 to 2026. Image source","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"zCo7wAonJY","urlSource":"./assets/LandIceAntarctica.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column":1},"end":{"line":118,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"antarcticicemass","identifier":"antarcticicemass","html_id":"antarcticicemass","enumerator":"4","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"H7K6KC9sYA"},{"type":"text","value":"4","key":"vszP5gfcIc"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"kAE3aw0r2m"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"pEUE7kAd7V"},{"type":"text","value":"Decline of Antarctic ice mass from 2002 to 2026. ","position":{"start":{"line":118,"column"